{
 "lead": "Frequency response is the graph of gain and phase against frequency. It answers a practical question: a circuit may work at 1 kHz but fail at 1 MHz. Capacitor and inductor reactances change with frequency, so the output changes too. The RC circuit gives a gentle first-order cutoff. The series RLC circuit gives a sharp band-pass peak. These two shapes sit inside radio tuning, audio filters and every phone's radio front end.",
 "prereq": "You need: phasors and impedance, and resonance, Q and bandwidth from <a href=\"#series-resonance\">series resonance</a> and <a href=\"#quality-factor-bandwidth-and-selectivity\">quality factor, bandwidth and selectivity</a>.",
 "sections": [
  {
   "h3": "The transfer function and the RC low-pass",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "Write the input as a phasor V<sub>in</sub> and the output as V<sub>out</sub>. The transfer function is H(jω) = V<sub>out</sub>/V<sub>in</sub>. It is a complex number at each frequency. Its size |H| is the gain; its angle is the phase shift. Gain in decibels is 20 log₁₀|H|. A gain of 1 is 0 dB. A gain of 0.707 is −3 dB."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "For the RC low-pass, take the output across C. The input divider gives H(jω) = (1/(jωC)) / (R + 1/(jωC)). Multiply top and bottom by jωC: H(jω) = 1/(1 + jωRC)."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "<b>H(jω) = 1 / (1 + jωRC),  |H| = 1 / √(1 + (ωRC)²),  φ = −arctan(ωRC)</b>"
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "Here ω is angular frequency in rad/s; R is resistance in Ω; C is capacitance in F; |H| is gain; φ is output phase minus input phase in degrees."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "The cutoff is where ωRC = 1. Define ω<sub>c</sub> = 1/(RC) and f<sub>c</sub> = 1/(2πRC). At this frequency |H| = 1/√2 = 0.707 and φ = −45°. The output lags the input. Below f<sub>c</sub> the gain is near 1. Above f<sub>c</sub> the gain falls. Far above f<sub>c</sub>, |H| ≈ 1/(ωRC), so every tenfold rise in frequency drops the gain by a factor 10. That is −20 dB per decade."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "The first-order RL low-pass has the same shape with time constant L/R. Take output across R with input across R and L in series: H = 1/(1 + jωL/R). Replace RC by L/R and the formulas carry over."
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "h3": "The RC high-pass and first-order symmetry",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "Take the output across R. The divider gives H(jω) = R / (R + 1/(jωC)) = jωRC / (1 + jωRC). Its magnitude is |H| = ωRC / √(1 + (ωRC)²) and its phase is φ = 90° − arctan(ωRC). At cutoff ω = ω<sub>c</sub>, |H| = 0.707 and φ = +45°. Far below cutoff |H| ≈ ωRC, so gain rises by 20 dB per decade. Far above cutoff |H| ≈ 1. The low-pass and high-pass magnitudes satisfy |H<sub>LP</sub>|² + |H<sub>HP</sub>|² = 1 for the same R and C."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "<b>H(jω) = jωRC / (1 + jωRC),  |H| = ωRC / √(1 + (ωRC)²),  φ = 90° − arctan(ωRC)</b>"
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "Here ω<sub>c</sub> = 1/(RC) is the cutoff; at ω = ω<sub>c</sub> the gain is 0.707 and the phase is +45°."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "A high-pass RC removes DC and low-frequency hum. In an audio coupling circuit it passes voice or music while blocking a steady offset. The same first-order shape appears in an RL high-pass when the output is across L."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "At f = 0.1 f<sub>c</sub> the high-pass gain is 0.0995 (−20 dB); at f = 10 f<sub>c</sub> it is 0.995 (−0.04 dB). So one decade below cutoff it passes about a tenth of the input, and one decade above it passes almost all of it."
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "h3": "Series RLC band-pass from the resistor voltage",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "Connect R, L and C in series and take the output across R. The input phasor sees impedance Z = R + j(ωL − 1/(ωC)). The transfer function is H(jω) = R / Z = 1 / [1 + j(ωL/R − 1/(ωCR))]. Define ω₀ = 1/√(LC), the resonant frequency, and Q = ω₀L/R = 1/(ω₀CR). Then H(jω) = 1 / [1 + jQ(ω/ω₀ − ω₀/ω)]."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "<b>|H| = 1 / √(1 + Q²(ω/ω₀ − ω₀/ω)²),  φ = −arctan[Q(ω/ω₀ − ω₀/ω)]</b>"
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "Here ω₀ is the resonant angular frequency in rad/s; Q is the series quality factor; |H| is gain; φ is phase in degrees."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "At ω = ω₀, the imaginary part is zero, so |H| = 1 and φ = 0°. The resistor voltage equals the input. Below resonance φ tends to +90°; above resonance φ tends to −90°. The curve has a peak at f₀. The half-power points are where |H| = 1/√2 = 0.707. They satisfy Q(ω/ω₀ − ω₀/ω) = ±1. Their separation is the bandwidth: Δω = ω₀/Q, or Δf = f₀/Q. A high Q gives a narrow, sharp band-pass; a low Q gives a wide, gentle one."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "Use this circuit as a frequency-selective filter. A TV tuner or radio front end picks one channel by tuning f₀ and fixing Q. The antenna may carry many stations; the band-pass passes the wanted band and rejects the rest. A narrow bandwidth means high Q and sharp selection, but it also means the tuning must be accurate (<a href=\"#quality-factor-bandwidth-and-selectivity\">quality factor, bandwidth and selectivity</a>)."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "The figure draws both shapes for the worked example on a log frequency axis: the RC low-pass and high-pass with R = 1 kΩ and C = 100 nF cross at −3 dB at f<sub>c</sub> = 1591.5 Hz, and the band-pass with f₀ = 1 kHz and Q = 10 peaks at 0 dB and is down 3 dB at about 950 Hz and 1050 Hz."
    }
   ]
  }
 ],
