 "examples": [
  {
   "question": "An RC low-pass filter has R = 1 kΩ and C = 100 nF. (a) Find f<sub>c</sub>. (b) Find gain and phase at 1 kHz. (c) A series RLC band-pass has f₀ = 1 kHz and Q = 10: find its bandwidth and its gain at 1.2 kHz.",
   "steps": [
    "Convert: R = 1 kΩ = 1000 Ω, C = 100 nF = 100 × 10⁻⁹ F, f = 1 kHz = 1000 Hz.",
    "Cutoff: f<sub>c</sub> = 1/(2πRC) = 1/(2π × 1000 × 100 × 10⁻⁹) = 1/(6.2832 × 10⁻⁴) = 1591.5 Hz.",
    "Frequency ratio: f/f<sub>c</sub> = 1000/1591.5 = 0.6283.",
    "Gain: |H| = 1/√(1 + 0.6283²) = 1/√(1.3948) = 1/1.1810 = 0.8467 ≈ 0.847.",
    "Phase: φ = −arctan(0.6283) = −32.14° ≈ −32.1°.",
    "RLC bandwidth: BW = f₀/Q = 1000/10 = 100 Hz. Approximate half-power points: 950 Hz and 1050 Hz.",
    "Gain at 1.2 kHz: ω/ω₀ − ω₀/ω = 1.2 − 1/1.2 = 0.3667; Q × 0.3667 = 3.667; |H| = 1/√(1 + 3.667²) = 1/3.801 = 0.263, about −11.6 dB."
   ],
   "answer": "f<sub>c</sub> ≈ 1591.5 Hz; at 1 kHz the gain is 0.847 (−1.44 dB) with phase −32.1°; the band-pass has BW = 100 Hz and passes only 0.263 of the input at 1.2 kHz.",
   "check": "import math\nR = 1000.0\nC = 100e-9\nf = 1000.0\nfc = 1/(2*math.pi*R*C)\ngain = 1/math.sqrt(1 + (f/fc)**2)\nphase = -math.degrees(math.atan(f/fc))\nf0 = 1000.0\nQ = 10.0\nbw = f0/Q\nprint(round(fc, 1))\nprint(round(gain, 3))\nprint(round(phase, 1))\nprint(round(bw, 1))",
   "values": [
    1591.5,
    0.847,
    -32.1,
    100.0
   ],
   "output": ""
  }
 ],
 "traps": [
  "−3 dB is gain 0.707, not 0.5. Half-power means power falls to half, and power goes as gain squared.",
  "Do not mix ω in rad/s with f in Hz. Convert with ω = 2πf before using ω<sub>c</sub> = 1/RC.",
  "In a series RLC band-pass, the voltage across R peaks at f₀, but the voltage across C peaks below f₀ and across L peaks above f₀. The examiner may ask which output is taken.",
  "The formula BW = f₀/Q uses the half-power bandwidth of the band-pass. A first-order RC has no Q and no such bandwidth; its cutoff is a single −3 dB point.",
  "Phase signs: RC low-pass output lags (−φ), RC high-pass output leads (+φ) at the same frequency. At cutoff the magnitudes are both 0.707, but the phases are −45° and +45°."
 ],
 "quiz": [
  {
   "level": "remember",
   "q": "State the cutoff frequency and the gain at cutoff for a first-order RC low-pass filter.",
   "marks": 2,
   "a": "f<sub>c</sub> = 1/(2πRC). At f<sub>c</sub>, |H| = 1/√2 = 0.707, which is −3 dB."
  },
  {
   "level": "understand",
   "q": "Why does the gain of an RC low-pass fall as frequency rises?",
   "marks": 3,
   "a": "The capacitor reactance 1/(ωC) falls. The capacitor looks more and more like a short, so less input voltage appears across it. In H = 1/(1 + jωRC), the denominator grows with ω."
  },
  {
   "level": "apply",
